## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical I/O scheduling knowledge, correctness, and minimal code for a common OS component.

## Key Concepts
- SCAN elevator algorithm
- Head movement and direction reversal
- Service order derivation from a head position
- Edge cases: empty queue, all requests on one side

## Code Example
```c
// toy SCAN scheduler (upward)
int scan_schedule(int head, int req[], int n, int out[]) {
  int up[n], dn[n], upn=0, dnn=0, k=0;
  for (int i=0;i<n;i++) if (req[i] >= head) up[upn++] = req[i]; else dn[dnn++] = req[i];
  qsort(up, upn, sizeof(int), cmp_asc);
  qsort(dn, dnn, sizeof(int), cmp_desc);
  for (int i=0;i<upn;i++) out[k++] = up[i];
  for (int i=0;i<dnn;i++) out[k++] = dn[i];
  return k; // number of entries
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt SCAN for multiple disks or priority requests?
- How would you test correctness with random traces and measure average seek distance?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of probabilistic scheduling, fairness, and simple kernel-like data paths without overwhelming complexity.

## Key Concepts
- Lottery scheduling basics; per-process tickets; cumulative distribution; random number generation; blocking/wake interactions.
- Data structures: ready list with ticket counts; total_tickets; small RNG; race-free updates.
- Testing: compare empirical waiting time and starvation under IO-bound vs CPU-bound mixes.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int pid;
  int tickets;
  int state; // READY/BLOCKED
} proc_t;

static uint32_t rng = 12345;
static uint32_t lcg(void){ rng = 1664525u*rng + 1013904223u; return rng; }

proc_t* select_next(proc_t** procs, int n){
  int total = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY) total += procs[i]->tickets;
  if(total<=0) return NULL;
  int r = lcg() % total + 1;
  int acc = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY){ acc += procs[i]->tickets; if(acc >= r) return procs[i]; }
  return NULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple CPUs and true parallel dispatch?
- How would you prevent ticket manipulation by a misbehaving process and ensure numerical robustness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests memory-management basics: forking, page sharing, and Copy-On-Write semantics, plus race-free fault handling and TLB coherence.

## Key Concepts
- Copy-On-Write (COW)
- Page tables and PTE flags (RO, Writable, Present)
- Atomic update to mappings and per-page refcounts
- TLB flushing and race avoidance on faults
- Testing with concurrent writes and rapid forking

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-skeleton for a COW fault handler
void handle_cow_fault(Process *p, void *fault_addr) {
  Page *shared = get_page(p, fault_addr);
  if (!shared->cow) return; // unexpected
  Page *private = alloc_page();
  copy_page(private, shared->addr);
  // atomically remap fault_addr to private with W permissions
  update_pte(p, fault_addr, private, WRITE);
  decrement_ref(shared);
  flush_tlb(p, fault_addr);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for race conditions between two forks touching the same page?
- How would you extend to multi-threaded processes and NUMA-aware allocations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of page fault handling, page cache coordination, IO paths, and race condition handling under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Page fault handling workflow: detection, frame allocation, IO vs zero-fill
- Per-page locking and CAS-based inflight signaling to serialize IO
- PTE updates, TLB invalidation, and waking waiters
- Throughput considerations under concurrent faults

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of per-page fault handling (pseudo-C)
typedef struct { int inflight; void* frame; int swapped; } Page;
void handle_fault(Page* p) {
  if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&p->inflight, 0, 1) == 0) {
    p->frame = allocate_frame();
    if (p->swapped) read_from_storage(p->frame);
    else zero_fill(p->frame);
    update_pte(p, p->frame);
    invalidate_tlb();
    wake_waiters(p);
    p->inflight = 0;
  } else {
    wait_for_completion(p);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness with multiple concurrent faulting threads?
- What are potential race conditions besides inflight handling, and how would you mitigate them?

